From c56c75277b8d02bb18831851d42e2e759760b728 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Fri, 3 Apr 2026 08:46:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] force HWC power-off during display init to fix MTK
 boot-black-screen

on MTK devices like the blackview shark 8 (MT6789), the bootloader
leaves the display powered on (HWC mode 2).  the kernel's
disable_unused clock framework then kills the DSI and display clocks
before userspace starts.  the hardware is off but the HWC's internal
state still says mode 2.

during initializeDisplays(), the OFF request is skipped because SF's
internal state already reads OFF.  force the DisplayDevice state to
ON first so setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(OFF) runs through the full
power-off path including scheduler notification, pacesetter update,
and vsync teardown.  without this, the scheduler and HWC have
inconsistent views of the display state, which prevents frame
composition after the subsequent power-on.

the ON request then runs a proper full OFF->ON transition through
the DRM/KMS path, re-enabling the display clocks and DSI output.
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index eb481b1e1c..1cb4ef231e 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -6060,8 +6060,21 @@ void SurfaceFlinger::initializeDisplays() {
         ftl::FakeGuard guard(mStateLock);
 
         // In case of a restart, ensure all displays are off.
+        // on some MTK devices the bootloader leaves the display on and the
+        // kernel's disable_unused clock framework kills the DSI clocks
+        // before userspace starts.  the HWC still thinks the display is
+        // powered (mode 2) while the hardware is actually off.  the
+        // setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(OFF) call below would normally be
+        // skipped because SF's internal state already reads OFF.  force
+        // the display state to ON first so the OFF request runs through
+        // the full power-off path including scheduler and vsync teardown,
+        // then the subsequent ON does a proper full power-on sequence.
         for (const auto& [id, display] : mPhysicalDisplays) {
-            setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(getDisplayDeviceLocked(id), hal::PowerMode::OFF);
+            const auto dev = getDisplayDeviceLocked(id);
+            if (dev->getPowerMode() == hal::PowerMode::OFF) {
+                dev->setPowerMode(hal::PowerMode::ON);
+            }
+            setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(dev, hal::PowerMode::OFF);
         }
 
         // Power on all displays. The primary display is first, so becomes the active display. Also,
